1
00:00:00,318 --> 00:00:03,279
Chapter 12.

2
00:00:03,279 --> 00:00:08,879
My mind grew very uneasy on the subject of the pale young gentleman. The more I thought of the

3
00:00:08,879 --> 00:00:13,879
fight, and recalled the pale young gentleman on his back in various stages of puffy and

4
00:00:13,879 --> 00:00:20,318
incrimsoned countenance, the more certain it appeared that something would be done to me. I

5
00:00:20,318 --> 00:00:26,120
felt that the pale young gentleman’s blood was on my head, and that the Law would avenge it.

6
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:31,120
Without having any definite idea of the penalties I had incurred, it was clear to me that village

7
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:36,519
boys could not go stalking about the country, ravaging the houses of gentlefolks and pitching

8
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:43,359
into the studious youth of England, without laying themselves open to severe punishment. For some

9
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:48,599
days, I even kept close at home, and looked out at the kitchen door with the greatest caution and

10
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:54,318
trepidation before going on an errand, lest the officers of the County Jail should pounce upon

11
00:00:54,318 --> 00:01:00,840
me. The pale young gentleman’s nose had stained my trousers, and I tried to wash out that evidence

12
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:07,359
of my guilt in the dead of night. I had cut my knuckles against the pale young gentleman’s teeth,

13
00:01:07,359 --> 00:01:12,799
and I twisted my imagination into a thousand tangles, as I devised incredible ways of

14
00:01:12,799 --> 00:01:18,680
accounting for that damnatory circumstance when I should be haled before the Judges.

15
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:22,719
When the day came round for my return to the scene of the deed of violence,

16
00:01:22,719 --> 00:01:29,439
my terrors reached their height. Whether myrmidons of Justice, especially sent down from London,

17
00:01:29,439 --> 00:01:34,718
would be lying in ambush behind the gate;—whether Miss Havisham, preferring to take personal

18
00:01:34,719 --> 00:01:40,159
vengeance for an outrage done to her house, might rise in those grave-clothes of hers,

19
00:01:40,159 --> 00:01:47,359
draw a pistol, and shoot me dead:—whether suborned boys—a numerous band of mercenaries—might be

20
00:01:47,359 --> 00:01:54,238
engaged to fall upon me in the brewery, and cuff me until I was no more;—it was high testimony to

21
00:01:54,238 --> 00:01:59,318
my confidence in the spirit of the pale young gentleman, that I never imagined him accessory

22
00:01:59,318 --> 00:02:05,638
to these retaliations; they always came into my mind as the acts of injudicious relatives of his,

23
00:02:05,639 --> 00:02:11,639
goaded on by the state of his visage and an indignant sympathy with the family features.

24
00:02:11,639 --> 00:02:18,359
However, go to Miss Havisham’s I must, and go I did. And behold! nothing came of

25
00:02:18,359 --> 00:02:24,000
the late struggle. It was not alluded to in any way, and no pale young gentleman was to

26
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,040
be discovered on the premises. I found the same gate open, and I explored the garden,

27
00:02:29,719 --> 00:02:35,238
and even looked in at the windows of the detached house; but my view was suddenly stopped by the

28
00:02:35,238 --> 00:02:41,478
closed shutters within, and all was lifeless. Only in the corner where the combat had taken

29
00:02:41,478 --> 00:02:46,879
place could I detect any evidence of the young gentleman’s existence. There were

30
00:02:46,879 --> 00:02:53,799
traces of his gore in that spot, and I covered them with garden-mould from the eye of man.

31
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:59,040
On the broad landing between Miss Havisham’s own room and that other room in which the long

32
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:05,159
table was laid out, I saw a garden-chair,—a light chair on wheels, that you pushed from

33
00:03:05,159 --> 00:03:11,279
behind. It had been placed there since my last visit, and I entered, that same day,

34
00:03:11,279 --> 00:03:16,519
on a regular occupation of pushing Miss Havisham in this chair (when she was tired of walking with

35
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:24,000
her hand upon my shoulder) round her own room, and across the landing, and round the other room.

36
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,639
Over and over and over again, we would make these journeys, and sometimes they would last as long as

37
00:03:29,639 --> 00:03:37,279
three hours at a stretch. I insensibly fall into a general mention of these journeys as numerous,

38
00:03:37,279 --> 00:03:42,159
because it was at once settled that I should return every alternate day at noon for these

39
00:03:42,159 --> 00:03:48,238
purposes, and because I am now going to sum up a period of at least eight or ten months.

40
00:03:48,238 --> 00:03:53,519
As we began to be more used to one another, Miss Havisham talked more to me, and asked

41
00:03:53,520 --> 00:04:00,199
me such questions as what had I learnt and what was I going to be? I told her I was going to be

42
00:04:00,199 --> 00:04:05,760
apprenticed to Joe, I believed; and I enlarged upon my knowing nothing and wanting to know

43
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:13,079
everything, in the hope that she might offer some help towards that desirable end. But she did not;

44
00:04:13,079 --> 00:04:18,359
on the contrary, she seemed to prefer my being ignorant. Neither did she ever give me any

45
00:04:18,359 --> 00:04:25,638
money,—or anything but my daily dinner,—nor ever stipulate that I should be paid for my services.

46
00:04:25,639 --> 00:04:30,520
Estella was always about, and always let me in and out, but never told me I might kiss her

47
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:38,279
again. Sometimes, she would coldly tolerate me; sometimes, she would condescend to me;

48
00:04:38,279 --> 00:04:44,000
sometimes, she would be quite familiar with me; sometimes, she would tell me energetically

49
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,079
that she hated me. Miss Havisham would often ask me in a whisper, or when we were alone,

50
00:04:50,079 --> 00:04:55,359
“Does she grow prettier and prettier, Pip?” And when I said yes (for indeed she did),

51
00:04:55,359 --> 00:05:01,519
would seem to enjoy it greedily. Also, when we played at cards Miss Havisham would look on,

52
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:06,959
with a miserly relish of Estella’s moods, whatever they were. And sometimes, when her

53
00:05:06,959 --> 00:05:12,238
moods were so many and so contradictory of one another that I was puzzled what to say or do,

54
00:05:12,238 --> 00:05:16,718
Miss Havisham would embrace her with lavish fondness, murmuring something in her ear that

55
00:05:16,719 --> 00:05:23,840
sounded like “Break their hearts my pride and hope, break their hearts and have no mercy!”

56
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:30,639
There was a song Joe used to hum fragments of at the forge, of which the burden was Old Clem. This

57
00:05:30,639 --> 00:05:37,199
was not a very ceremonious way of rendering homage to a patron saint, but I believe Old Clem stood

58
00:05:37,199 --> 00:05:43,920
in that relation towards smiths. It was a song that imitated the measure of beating upon iron,

59
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:50,600
and was a mere lyrical excuse for the introduction of Old Clem’s respected name. Thus, you were to

60
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:58,920
hammer boys round—Old Clem! With a thump and a sound—Old Clem! Beat it out, beat it out—Old

61
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:06,559
Clem! With a clink for the stout—Old Clem! Blow the fire, blow the fire—Old Clem! Roaring dryer,

62
00:06:06,559 --> 00:06:13,238
soaring higher—Old Clem! One day soon after the appearance of the chair, Miss Havisham suddenly

63
00:06:13,238 --> 00:06:19,519
saying to me, with the impatient movement of her fingers, “There, there, there! Sing!” I was

64
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:25,439
surprised into crooning this ditty as I pushed her over the floor. It happened so to catch her

65
00:06:25,439 --> 00:06:32,679
fancy that she took it up in a low brooding voice as if she were singing in her sleep. After that,

66
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:38,920
it became customary with us to have it as we moved about, and Estella would often join in; though

67
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:44,799
the whole strain was so subdued, even when there were three of us, that it made less noise in the

68
00:06:44,799 --> 00:06:51,478
grim old house than the lightest breath of wind. What could I become with these surroundings? How

69
00:06:51,478 --> 00:06:57,558
could my character fail to be influenced by them? Is it to be wondered at if my thoughts were dazed,

70
00:06:57,559 --> 00:07:03,799
as my eyes were, when I came out into the natural light from the misty yellow rooms?

71
00:07:03,799 --> 00:07:08,478
Perhaps I might have told Joe about the pale young gentleman, if I had not previously

72
00:07:08,478 --> 00:07:14,638
been betrayed into those enormous inventions to which I had confessed. Under the circumstances,

73
00:07:14,639 --> 00:07:19,398
I felt that Joe could hardly fail to discern in the pale young gentleman, an appropriate

74
00:07:19,398 --> 00:07:25,799
passenger to be put into the black velvet coach; therefore, I said nothing of him. Besides,

75
00:07:25,799 --> 00:07:30,079
that shrinking from having Miss Havisham and Estella discussed, which had come upon me in the

76
00:07:30,079 --> 00:07:38,318
beginning, grew much more potent as time went on. I reposed complete confidence in no one but Biddy;

77
00:07:38,318 --> 00:07:43,840
but I told poor Biddy everything. Why it came natural to me to do so, and why Biddy

78
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:51,359
had a deep concern in everything I told her, I did not know then, though I think I know now.

79
00:07:51,359 --> 00:07:57,159
Meanwhile, councils went on in the kitchen at home, fraught with almost insupportable

80
00:07:57,159 --> 00:08:04,920
aggravation to my exasperated spirit. That ass, Pumblechook, used often to come over of a night

81
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:11,238
for the purpose of discussing my prospects with my sister; and I really do believe (to this hour

82
00:08:11,238 --> 00:08:16,359
with less penitence than I ought to feel), that if these hands could have taken a linchpin out

83
00:08:16,359 --> 00:08:23,238
of his chaise-cart, they would have done it. The miserable man was a man of that confined stolidity

84
00:08:23,238 --> 00:08:29,279
of mind, that he could not discuss my prospects without having me before him,—as it were,

85
00:08:29,279 --> 00:08:35,079
to operate upon,—and he would drag me up from my stool (usually by the collar) where I was quiet

86
00:08:35,079 --> 00:08:40,879
in a corner, and, putting me before the fire as if I were going to be cooked, would begin by saying,

87
00:08:40,879 --> 00:08:45,920
“Now, Mum, here is this boy! Here is this boy which you brought up by hand. Hold up your head,

88
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:52,920
boy, and be forever grateful unto them which so did do. Now, Mum, with respections to this boy!”

89
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:57,600
And then he would rumple my hair the wrong way,—which from my earliest remembrance,

90
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:02,840
as already hinted, I have in my soul denied the right of any fellow-creature to do,—and would

91
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:09,959
hold me before him by the sleeve,—a spectacle of imbecility only to be equalled by himself.

92
00:09:09,959 --> 00:09:15,159
Then, he and my sister would pair off in such nonsensical speculations about Miss Havisham,

93
00:09:15,159 --> 00:09:20,398
and about what she would do with me and for me, that I used to want—quite painfully—to

94
00:09:20,398 --> 00:09:27,558
burst into spiteful tears, fly at Pumblechook, and pummel him all over. In these dialogues, my sister

95
00:09:27,559 --> 00:09:32,199
spoke to me as if she were morally wrenching one of my teeth out at every reference; while

96
00:09:32,199 --> 00:09:38,638
Pumblechook himself, self-constituted my patron, would sit supervising me with a depreciatory eye,

97
00:09:38,639 --> 00:09:44,680
like the architect of my fortunes who thought himself engaged on a very unremunerative job.

98
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:50,840
In these discussions, Joe bore no part. But he was often talked at, while they were in progress,

99
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:57,238
by reason of Mrs. Joe’s perceiving that he was not favourable to my being taken from the forge.

100
00:09:57,238 --> 00:10:03,760
I was fully old enough now to be apprenticed to Joe; and when Joe sat with the poker on his knees

101
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:09,600
thoughtfully raking out the ashes between the lower bars, my sister would so distinctly construe

102
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:15,439
that innocent action into opposition on his part, that she would dive at him, take the poker out of

103
00:10:15,439 --> 00:10:24,478
his hands, shake him, and put it away. There was a most irritating end to every one of these debates.

104
00:10:24,478 --> 00:10:29,759
All in a moment, with nothing to lead up to it, my sister would stop herself in a yawn,

105
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:35,238
and catching sight of me as it were incidentally, would swoop upon me with, “Come! there’s enough of

106
00:10:35,238 --> 00:10:41,159
you! You get along to bed; you’ve given trouble enough for one night, I hope!” As if I had

107
00:10:41,159 --> 00:10:47,398
besought them as a favour to bother my life out. We went on in this way for a long time, and it

108
00:10:47,398 --> 00:10:51,718
seemed likely that we should continue to go on in this way for a long time,

109
00:10:51,719 --> 00:10:57,439
when one day Miss Havisham stopped short as she and I were walking, she leaning on my shoulder;

110
00:10:57,439 --> 00:11:01,799
and said with some displeasure,— “You are growing tall, Pip!”

111
00:11:01,799 --> 00:11:05,559
I thought it best to hint, through the medium of a meditative look,

112
00:11:05,559 --> 00:11:10,559
that this might be occasioned by circumstances over which I had no control.

113
00:11:10,559 --> 00:11:16,639
She said no more at the time; but she presently stopped and looked at me again; and presently

114
00:11:16,639 --> 00:11:23,840
again; and after that, looked frowning and moody. On the next day of my attendance,

115
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:28,159
when our usual exercise was over, and I had landed her at her dressing-table,

116
00:11:28,159 --> 00:11:32,318
she stayed me with a movement of her impatient fingers:—

117
00:11:32,318 --> 00:11:35,519
“Tell me the name again of that blacksmith of yours.”

118
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:37,799
“Joe Gargery, ma’am.”

119
00:11:37,799 --> 00:11:41,238
“Meaning the master you were to be apprenticed to?”

120
00:11:41,238 --> 00:11:43,040
“Yes, Miss Havisham.”

121
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:46,680
“You had better be apprenticed at once. Would Gargery come here with you,

122
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:49,520
and bring your indentures, do you think?”

123
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:54,799
I signified that I had no doubt he would take it as an honour to be asked.

124
00:11:54,799 --> 00:11:56,760
“Then let him come.”

125
00:11:56,760 --> 00:11:58,440
“At any particular time, Miss Havisham?”

126
00:11:59,478 --> 00:12:06,120
“There, there! I know nothing about times. Let him come soon, and come along with you.”

127
00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:10,680
When I got home at night, and delivered this message for Joe, my sister “went on

128
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:17,318
the Rampage,” in a more alarming degree than at any previous period. She asked me and Joe whether

129
00:12:17,318 --> 00:12:23,040
we supposed she was door-mats under our feet, and how we dared to use her so, and what company we

130
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:29,478
graciously thought she was fit for? When she had exhausted a torrent of such inquiries, she threw

131
00:12:29,478 --> 00:12:35,238
a candlestick at Joe, burst into a loud sobbing, got out the dustpan,—which was always a very bad

132
00:12:35,238 --> 00:12:42,159
sign,—put on her coarse apron, and began cleaning up to a terrible extent. Not satisfied with a dry

133
00:12:42,159 --> 00:12:47,959
cleaning, she took to a pail and scrubbing-brush, and cleaned us out of house and home, so that we

134
00:12:47,959 --> 00:12:53,000
stood shivering in the back-yard. It was ten o’clock at night before we ventured to creep

135
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:59,318
in again, and then she asked Joe why he hadn’t married a Negress Slave at once? Joe offered no

136
00:12:59,318 --> 00:13:05,199
answer, poor fellow, but stood feeling his whisker and looking dejectedly at me, as if he thought it

137
00:13:05,199 --> 00:13:07,991
really might have been a better speculation. Chapter 13.
